On 2007-03-06, masquerade808@gmail.com <masquerade808@gmail.com> wrote:
> I've heard mentions in many different places of intentionally avoiding
> fights and decreasing your own level as a strategy. This is a newbie
> question, but what is the advantage of limiting or decreasing your
> level, what classes should do it, and when?

"This tin is full of worms. Eat it?"

Having a low experience level has two main advantages:

 * Easier monsters will be generated.
 * It costs much less to donate money to priests for divine protection.

Gaining experience levels has these advantages:

 * More HP.
 * More Pw.
 * More skill slots.
 * Improved spell failure rates.
 * For some classes, more intrinsics. (Valks get speed, monks get PR,
   &c. &c.)

I'm probably forgetting some more minor things.

There is a popular strategy called the "Protection Run" or "Protection
Racket" or "Healer's Gambit" wherein you start a character, often a
gnomish healer (who starts with healing potions, poison resistance, a wand
of sleep, and most monsters in the Mines generated peaceful), and try to
get that character to the priest in Minetown at low XL, usually not higher
than 3. You then credit clone the Minetown shops with your pet, getting
up to 10,000 or so zorkmids, and buy your armor class down to 0 or so.
It's certainly nice when it works; people have argued a lot recently about
whether or not it's a good idea, but leaving that question entirely aside,
it can be fun to try.

The more interesting question, in my mind, is how valuable getting easier
monsters is. As a strawman, I went into wizard mode and #levelchanged to
30 on DL 1, and then descended through DL4. The toughest monster I saw
was a werejackal. However, even an XL1 character on DL20 is going to be
facing some decently toothy opponents, including such crowd favorites
as mumakil and soldier ants. 

This doesn't mean that getting to XL30 on DL1 is necessarily a good
idea, though.  Higher-level monsters do more damage, even relative to
maximum hit points in some cases (minotaurs are particularly abusive).
It's important, before being too high-level, to get better weapons,
armor, intrinsics, spells, and equipment that will help you handle the
additional threats posed by more powerful monsters. It is for this reason
that some players will recommend that you keep your experience level down.

However, I believe there is a catch-22. Where do you get those better
items and abilities? From experience levels (better HP, more intrinsics,
easier to cast spells) and from higher-level monsters (no one ever wore
newt scale mail, and deathdrops of more powerful monsters are usually
more powerful). Now, you can get some very good equipment from Minetown
and from letting your pet kill monsters, but unless your pet is really
strong and you're really good at avoiding damage, you'll eventually want
the cushion that extra HP provides. 

Also, some of the more useful intrinsic-granting monsters (my personal
favorite example is unicorns, who also give that all-important horn)
won't be generated until you reach a reasonable level. So unless you
have other sources of fire, cold, sleep, shock, and poison resistance,
various traps, wands, and projectiles will be more dangerous to you at
very low experience level than they would otherwise be.

So, if you're not trying to keep your XL down in order to donate to
priests early in the game, I'd recommend that you not worry about your
XL, and let it grow naturally. For most players, the advantages of a
low XL are outweighed by the advantages of a higher XL. I usually want
my XL to be close to my dungeon level until I hit 8 or so, and then I
just stop paying attention. That's not a hard and fast rule, though.

Hope this helps,

